# Expectation record for 07_variadics/001_int_args.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_int_args
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Integer variadic arguments of three widths retrieved in order
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every va_arg names exactly the type the caller supplied, and no list is read past the
number of arguments actually passed.  Each list created by va_start is ended exactly once
by va_end in the same function.  All sums stay inside the range of their type.  Each
retrieval is exercised twice, once from literal arguments and once from arguments
originating in volatile storage.  <stdarg.h> is included because a variadic function cannot
be written without it and it is in both compilers' freestanding sets; printf is still
hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
